      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my realtor just mentioned something about needing an escrow account for this house i'm looking at in toronto. i've never bought a place before so i'm kinda lost. can u explain what escrow actually is and why it's even used in the closing process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow, in the context of real estate, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met. It's a way to ensure that all parties involved in a transaction, like the buyer and seller, fulfill their obligations. Think of it like a temporary \"goblin's hoard\" where money is set aside until the deal is finalized. You see, in the old days, goblins used to stash their treasure in hidden caves, and it was only when they were satisfied with the terms that they'd release the loot. Similarly, escrow accounts act as a safeguard, releasing funds only when the conditions are met, like the buyer securing financing or the seller completing repairs.\n\nIn the case of a home purchase, escrow typically covers property taxes and insurance. The funds are set aside until the annual tax bills are paid and the insurance premiums are paid. It's a way to ensure that the buyer isn't left with a huge bill at the end of the year. The escrow account is usually managed by a third-party company, like a title company or a bank, and is monitored by the buyer and seller to ensure everything runs smoothly.\n\nNow, when it comes to the closing process, escrow plays a crucial role. It",
